Product Requirements Document

Dynamic Agent Factory | Local macOS Proof of Concept

Version 1.0  •  Status: Proposed  •  Date: October 17, 2026

Primary user: POC owner / developer  |  Development tool: Claude Code

1. Executive summary

Build a local Agent Factory that converts a natural-language problem or product requirements document (PRD) into a validated executable blueprint, dynamically assembles a LangGraph agent/workflow pipeline, executes it, and returns results with a complete trace. The factory should avoid requiring an engineer to handcraft business-specific agent pipelines for each new use case. Claude Code is the development assistant; the delivered factory must operate independently of Claude Code.

2. Problem and opportunity

Today, new agentic business problems often require engineers to design named agents, implement handoffs, connect tools, and build execution pipelines repeatedly. This POC tests whether a business user can describe desired outcomes and allow a governed factory to configure and run a fit-for-purpose workflow. It is intended to inform future enterprise implementation, not to deploy production banking workflows.

3. Goals, outcomes and non-goals

3.1 Goals

Accept natural-language prompts or uploaded text-based PRDs and identify tasks, dependencies, expected outputs and constraints.

Automatically select and compose serial, parallel, hub/subagent and maker–checker patterns, including hybrid graphs.

Generate a versioned, machine-readable executable architecture specification with required/optional context contracts, tool permissions, success criteria and stop conditions.

Validate, compile, execute, observe and retain distinct graphs for distinct PRDs.

Run the complete application locally on macOS; hosted model calls may be used with user authorization.

3.2 Explicit non-goals for Phase 1

Unrestricted self-modifying software or arbitrary model-generated Python execution.

Production-scale multi-tenant authentication, bank systems integration, or distributed deployment.

Autonomous external side effects without tool allowlisting and human approval.

Guaranteeing that generated agent plans or outputs are correct without evaluation.

4. Users and primary workflow

Primary persona: the POC owner, who enters a PRD, reviews the architecture the system proposes, approves execution, and inspects the result. Future persona: a business product partner drafting outcomes without writing pipeline code.

Core journey: Submit PRD → Analyze requirements → Decompose tasks → Select patterns and capabilities → Produce blueprint → Validate contracts and graph → Show graph and obtain approval → Compile and run → Validate/revise where needed → View results and trace → Persist artifacts.

5. Product scope and feature requirements

ID

Requirement

Priority

Acceptance criteria

FR-01

Text PRD input and problem prompt; upload local .md/.txt (PDF optional later).

Must

A user can submit a PRD and receive a unique run ID; missing objective is surfaced.

FR-02

Work Director extracts objective, tasks, dependencies, inputs, outputs and constraints.

Must

Structured analysis conforms to a validated Pydantic schema.

FR-03

Pattern selector supports four patterns and hybrids.

Must

Fixture PRDs produce correct parallel, serial, supervisor and bounded review flows.

FR-04

Versioned blueprint generator.

Must

Stores machine-readable task/agent specs, DAG edges, tool IDs, state contracts, policies and exit criteria.

FR-05

Blueprint validation before execution.

Must

Rejects cycles outside declared bounded loops, unresolved dependencies, missing mandatory data and unapproved tools.

FR-06

Dynamic LangGraph compiler and executor.

Must

At least three materially different PRDs compile to different executable graphs without case-specific agent code.

FR-07

Tool registry and skill catalog.

Must

Nodes can use only explicitly registered and permitted tools/skills; unavailable capabilities fail safely.

FR-08

Human approval gate.

Must

User can review and approve/reject a proposed run; restricted side effects require additional approvals.

FR-09

Execution trace, results and persistence.

Must

View graph, steps, status, outputs and failure reasons by run ID; restart restores persisted history.

FR-10

Maker–checker evaluation loop.

Must

Evaluation uses explicit rubric and max revisions; loops stop and report if criteria not met.

FR-11

Local Streamlit interface + FastAPI.

Must

User can submit, inspect, approve, run and export an output through localhost.

FR-12

Optional Chroma/RAG, Deep Agents delegation, generated skills and code.

Later

Demonstrated only after Phase 1; generated code sandboxed and approved.

6. Four required architecture patterns

Pattern

Rule for selection

Execution semantics

Parallel

Independent tasks with no required upstream context.

Fan-out; concurrent node execution; deterministic join and aggregation.

Serial

Downstream task depends on output from predecessor.

Dependency-ordered edges; schema validation at each handoff.

Hub + subagent

Decomposition requires specialized delegated investigation.

Supervisor delegates scoped sub-tasks; results are consolidated into shared state.

Maker–checker

Quality or factual verification requires revision.

Maker → checker → conditional revision up to max attempts; stop on success or limit.

7. System architecture

Frontend: Streamlit localhost:8501 to accept PRDs, visualize proposed execution graphs, show approval controls, progress and outputs. Backend/API: FastAPI localhost:8000 for submissions, blueprints, approvals, runs and results. Factory engine: LangGraph Work Director, requirements analyzer, pattern selector, blueprint generator and validator, dynamic graph compiler and executor. AI/model layer: LangChain model integration with Anthropic Claude API; optional Ollama local models. Agent capabilities: Deep Agents where delegated planning/specialist capabilities are warranted rather than for every task. Context: Pydantic input/output contracts, local files and SQLite storage, optional Chroma for skill/knowledge retrieval. Operations: structured local logs, execution and cost budgets, optional LangSmith tracing.

8. Logical interfaces and data contracts

8.1 Blueprint required fields

blueprint_id, version, objective, source_prd_hash, creation timestamp

tasks[]: id, role, responsibility, required_inputs, optional_inputs, output_schema, dependencies

execution plan: pattern and graph edge definitions; bounded loops; concurrency cap

capabilities: registered tools, referenced skills, model configuration and allowed operations

policies: approval requirements, step/retry/time limits and failure handling

quality: measurable criteria, checker rubric, final output schema, disposition on unmet quality threshold

8.2 Example API endpoints (design targets)

Method

Route

Expected outcome

POST

/prds

Create submission and analysis request

GET

/blueprints/{id}

Retrieve validated blueprint

POST

/blueprints/{id}/approve

Approve or reject a blueprint

POST

/runs

Start approved execution

GET

/runs/{id}

Get state, trace and results

GET

/health

Local readiness check

8.3 State and handoff behavior

Each agent consumes an explicitly validated input object and emits a validated output object. Mandatory context absence must result in a user-input interrupt or a safe failure, not fabricated values. Optional fields may be null. Fan-out branches store results under distinct keys to avoid conflicting state writes; aggregators merge their outputs predictably. Persist run state and checkpoints.

9. Security, governance and safety requirements

Synthetic or publicly shareable data only; do not use confidential bank data or personal API accounts for regulated information.

Bind the app to loopback interfaces by default; store secrets in environment variables and exclude them from source control.

Use allowlisted tool names, explicit filesystem scopes and resource budgets.

Do not execute model-generated Python on the host in Phase 1; later use isolation, static checks, automated tests and human approval.

Record user approvals, blueprint hash/version, tool invocations, model usage, evaluation outcomes and errors.

Escalate missing permissions or unavailable capabilities rather than silently substituting tools.

10. Nonfunctional requirements and target measures

Area

POC target / measure

Reliability

100% of invalid test blueprints rejected before execution in the curated negative-test suite.

Graph correctness

3/3 distinct demonstration PRDs yield validated, executable and appropriately different graphs.

Termination

All tests involving bounded maker–checker loops stop at the configured limit.

Observability

100% of demonstration runs show run ID, blueprint version, node status and final disposition.

Local usability

Documented setup on one macOS machine; frontend/API accessible on localhost.

Performance and cost

Measure graph planning latency, execution latency, API calls and token cost per demonstration; initial thresholds set after baseline.

Quality

Use scenario-specific rubrics; display both checker outcome and unmet criteria, without implying model self-evaluation guarantees correctness.

11. Delivery phases

Phase

Scope

Exit criterion

1 — Core proof

Work Director, Pydantic contracts, 4 patterns, validated blueprint, graph compiler, trusted tool registry, checkpoints, FastAPI, Streamlit, tests.

Three distinct PRDs executed end-to-end; demo produces reproducible traces and bounded failures.

2 — Capability expansion

Skill discovery, safe skill generation, Deep Agents specialization, controlled tool creation, context quality gating, optional Chroma.

Factory identifies missing capability and adds approved/validated capability to a run.

3 — Enterprise readiness

Policy-as-code, audit controls, reusable blueprint catalog, stronger evaluation, multi-user control plane, sandboxing and deployment strategy.

Architecture and controls are reviewable for enterprise adoption; not automatically production-certified.

12. UAT scenarios and definition of done

Scenario

Expected result

Parallel competitive analysis

Three independent research branches fan out and are joined for comparison and report.

Serial data transformation

Input validation precedes transformation; dependent stages do not run with absent mandatory outputs.

Hybrid analysis + maker–checker

Parallel inputs converge, draft is reviewed, bounded revision occurs, final quality disposition appears.

Missing mandatory context

Validation raises actionable missing-field error or interrupt; no downstream run.

Unapproved tool / code execution

Execution blocked and approval/audit event recorded.

Checker never approves

Loop terminates at revision limit; best available artifact is labeled as not meeting threshold.

App restart

Past runs/blueprint metadata remain queryable from local persistence.

Definition of done: All Must functional requirements demonstrated, test suite passes, three example PRDs execute without handwritten business-specific agents, generated graph view matches actual execution trace, setup instructions reproduce the POC locally, and known limitations are documented.

13. Dependencies, assumptions and risks

Assumption: Python 3.11+ is available on macOS, with local install rights and internet access for optional hosted models.

Dependency: Compatible, pinned LangChain/LangGraph/Deep Agents versions must be verified at build time.

Risk: Model-generated decomposition may miss dependencies; mitigate through structural graph validation, deterministic contracts and user approval.

Risk: Tool and skill generation can introduce unsafe code; defer arbitrary execution and enforce allowlists and sandboxing.

Risk: LLM output and checker assessments can be unreliable; measure against human-reviewed fixtures and retain transparent review status.

Risk: Anthropic API usage creates external data transfer and variable cost; use synthetic data, allow configurable model providers and track usage.

14. Open product decisions

Should the first release accept only text/Markdown PRDs, or should PDF parsing be required? Recommendation: text/Markdown first.

Is human approval mandatory for every proposed blueprint? Recommendation: yes for POC to evaluate factory decisions.

Which three representative PRDs should become golden test cases? Recommendation: competitor comparison, financial analysis with synthetic data, document transformation.

Should the factory generate new Python tools in Phase 1? Recommendation: no; trusted registry first, safe generation in Phase 2.

15. Implementation directive for Claude Code

Implement the core contracts, Work Director, blueprint validator, graph compiler and test harness before building the API or UI. Build the Phase 1 system as a local standalone Python application, not as a Claude Code session-dependent workflow. Use mocks for unit tests and at least one real model-backed end-to-end run. Supply README commands, requirements/lock file, .env.example, tests, example PRDs, architectural decisions and demo output artifacts.

